    "final_text": "I fixed all five problems in `src/inventory.js`. The full suite passes (15 tests, 0 failures). I didn't break the new tests against the old code to confirm they fail there.\n\n**Causes:**\n- **Overselling, negative `reserved`, double holds on retry, inflated `sold`:** Every operation read a counter, waited on the store, then wrote it back. Concurrent workers overwrote each other's updates, and two calls for the same order could both act on it.\n  - **Fix:** All operations that touch counters or orders now run one at a time through a single lock inside the service. This covers `addStock`, `reserve`, `commit`, `release`, `expire`, `available` and `snapshot`.\n  - **Trade-off:** It serializes everything in one process, so it will not protect against several separate service processes sharing one store.\n- **Bundle item stayed held after a rejection:** `reserve` held each item as it went, so a later item failing left the earlier ones held. It now checks every item first and holds only if all pass.\n- **`release` on a committed order:** It wasn't rejected, so it returned the sold units to `reserved` and drove it negative. It now throws `OrderStateError`, as the doc comment says.\n- **`expire()`:** It released every old order regardless of status, including committed ones. It now releases only orders still in `reserved`, and checks that status while holding the lock.\n\nThe public methods, arguments, return values and error classes are unchanged, and `src/store.js` is untouched.\n\nI added `tests/concurrency.test.js` with seven tests, one per cause plus concurrent `addStock` calls losing updates and commit racing release on one order.\n\nNothing has been committed.",
